.TH CVECTOR_PUSH_BACK 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cvector_push_back \- append an element to the end of the vector
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

ccol_retval_t cvector_push_back(cvec v, const void *new_elem);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Adds a new element to the end of the vector. The vector doubles its
capacity by itself when it is full. The function copies the element into
the vector. The copy uses an assignment that the library selects from the
element size.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v         Vector to append to
new_elem  Pointer to element to append (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success          - Element successfully appended
ccol_invalid_args     - new_elem is NULL
ccol_container_full   - Vector has reached ccol_max_elem_count
ccol_not_enough_memory - Capacity expansion failed
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Amortized O(1) complexity
The function copies the element into the vector
The capacity doubles when the vector is full (2x scaling factor)
Asserts if v is NULL
new_elem can safely point into the backing buffer of v. One example is
a pointer from cvector_data_ptr(3) or from cvector_at(3). A capacity
growth that this call starts does not invalidate such a pointer
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_pop_back (3),
.BR cvec_push (3)
